Q.1 When scaling infrastructure from a few servers to hundreds, 
what is the primary operational risk of relying on a 
"Command and Execute" (Traditional) management style?
Ans. The system lacks a central memory of intent, meaning no automatic
 recovery occurs if a process dies after the command executes.

Q.2 In a scenario where multiple engineers try to modify the cluster state simultaneously (e.g., scaling a deployment and updating an image), how does the API Server prevent conflicts and inconsistent states?
Ans. It acts as a single source of truth, validating and updating the stored state atomically, ensuring no overwrites occur.

Q.3 Why does the Kubernetes API Server not handle tasks like scheduling pods or restarting crashed containers directly?
Ans. To avoid creating a monolithic single point of failure and to allow for specialized, replaceable components (like custom schedulers).

Q.4 Which of the following best describes the specific "Reconciliation Loop" logic used by a Controller (e.g., ReplicaSet Controller)?
Ans. It continuously asks "What should exist?" and "What actually exists?", then acts to eliminate the difference.

Q.5 What is the precise, limited responsibility of the Scheduler component in Kubernetes?
Ans. It filters and scores nodes to find the best fit, then updates the Pod specification with the assigned nodeName.

Q.6 Why does Kubernetes use etcd for storage instead of keeping the cluster state in the API Server's memory?
Ans.etcd provides persistence (surviving crashes) and distributed consistency (ensuring all components see the same state).



Q.7 Why does Kubernetes use the "Pod" as the smallest deployable unit rather than scheduling individual containers directly?
Ans. To ensure that related containers always share the same node, network namespace, and lifecycle.

Q.8 When a Pod is deleted, Kubernetes follows a specific termination sequence. What is the correct order of operations?
Ans. SIGTERM is sent to all containers; if they are still running after the grace period, SIGKILL is sent.

Q.9 You have a batch processing workload defined with restartPolicy: OnFailure. If the container finishes its task and exits with code 0, what does the Kubelet do? 
Ans. It marks the Pod as "Succeeded" and does not restart the container.

Q.10 A Pod is configured with two Init Containers: setup-config and wait-for-db. If setup-config (the first one) fails and exits with a non-zero code, what happens next?
Ans. The Pod retries the setup-config container from the beginning.

Q.11 In a multi-container Pod, which of the following resources is ISOLATED (not shared) between containers by default?
Ans. The Filesystem (excluding mounted volumes).

Q.12 A container in your Pod has a memory limit of 256Mi. If the container process tries to consume 300Mi of memory, what action does Kubernetes take?
Ans. It terminates (OOMKills) the container.

Q.13 Your Pod is stuck in the Pending state. You run kubectl describe pod and see a FailedScheduling event. What is the most likely cause?
Ans. The cluster nodes do not have enough available resources (CPU/Memory) to meet the Pod's requests.

Q.14 A container is in CrashLoopBackOff. You need to see the error logs from the previous instance that crashed (not the currently restarting one). Which command should you use?
Ans. kubectl logs myapp -c container-name --previous

Q.15 You deploy a single Pod directly to a node to serve a production API. If that node crashes due to a hardware failure, what is the expected behavior of the Kubernetes control plane?
Ans. It takes no action; the Pod remains dead until a human manually recreates it or the node recovers.

Q.16 A ReplicaSet is configured with replicas: 5 and selector app: api. You currently have 5 running Pods managed by this ReplicaSet. If you manually create a new, separate Pod with the label app: api, what will the ReplicaSet controller do immediately?
Ans. It will adopt the new Pod and then terminate one Pod (either the new one or an old one) to return to 5 replicas.

Q.17     Virtual IPs & kube-proxy
Solved
A Service in Kubernetes is described as a "Virtual IP" that doesn't actually exist on a network interface. When a Pod sends a packet to a Service IP (ClusterIP), what mechanism on the node intercepts and redirects the traffic?
Ans. kube-proxy configures iptables rules to perform Destination NAT (DNAT).

Q.18 Q3. NodePort routing Behavior
Solved
You have a 3-node cluster and create a NodePort Service on port 30080. The Pods backing this Service happen to be running only on Node 3. If an external client connects to Node 1 on port 30080, what happens?
Ans. The connection succeeds; kube-proxy on Node 1 forwards the traffic internally to the Pod on Node 3.

Q.19 4. Ingress vs. LoadBalancer
Unsolved
You are designing a cluster for a startup that has 20 distinct HTTP microservices. Why would you typically choose an Ingress Controller over creating a standard LoadBalancer Service for each microservice?
Ans. To reduce cloud costs by sharing a single external Load Balancer and routing traffic based on Host/Path.

Q.20 Q5. The Complete Traffic Flow
Solved
In a standard production setup using an NGINX Ingress Controller, what is the correct hop-by-hop path for an external HTTP request reaching your application?
Ans. External User -> Cloud LB -> NodePort -> Ingress Controller Pod -> Service (ClusterIP) -> Application Pod.

Q.21 Headless Services
Solved
Why is a Headless Service (clusterIP: None) typically required when deploying a StatefulSet like a Database, rather than a standard Service?
Ans. To allow clients (or peers) to resolve the specific IP addresses of individual Pods directly via DNS.

Q.22 . Services in K8s
Solved
You are migrating a legacy application to Kubernetes. The app code tries to connect to a database at http://db-prod. However, the actual database still lives outside the cluster at db.legacy-corp.com. Which Service type allows you to map the internal name to the external DNS name?
Ans. ExternalName

Q.23  Network Policy
Solved
You apply a Network Policy that selects your backend Pods (podSelector: matchLabels: app: backend) but does not specify any ingress rules. What is the immediate effect on traffic to those Pods?
Ans. All incoming traffic to the backend Pods is blocked (Implicit Default Deny).

Q.24  Troubleshooting Signals
Solved
You are debugging a connectivity issue. When you run nc -z -v <ip> <port> from a client Pod, you receive a "Connection Timed Out" error. What is the most likely cause?
Ans. A Network Policy or Firewall is silently dropping the packets.

Q.25 The Scaling Layers
Solved
You are designing a scaling strategy for an e-commerce platform. You need a mechanism that can respond to sudden traffic spikes in seconds to minutes by adding more replicas of your application. Which scaling layer and tool is appropriate for this specific "fast response" requirement?
Ans. Layer 1: Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (Pod Count Level)

Q.26 Q10. Resolving Autoscaler Configurations
Solved
An engineering team configures a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) to scale a deployment based on a target of 70% CPU utilization. Simultaneously, they deploy a Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) set to "Auto" mode to manage the same deployment's CPU requests. What is the expected behavior of this cluster configuration?
Ans. The autoscalers will conflict and create instability, because the VPA continually alters the baseline resource requests that the HPA uses to calculate its utilization percentage.

Q.27 HPA Dependencies
Solved
You have deployed an application and configured an HPA to maintain 50% CPU utilization. However, when you run kubectl get hpa, the TARGETS column displays <unknown>/50%, and the HPA fails to scale. What is the most likely cause?
Ans. The Pods do not have Resource Requests defined in their manifest.

Q.28 . VPA Operation Modes
Solved
You have an existing production deployment with undersized pods that are constantly throttling. You want to implement Vertical Pod Autoscaling (VPA) to automatically evict these pods and recreate them with correct sizing. Which updateMode must be configured?
Ans. "Auto"

Q.29 HPA & VPA Conflict
Solved
What is the reason for warning against running HPA and VPA on the same metric (e.g., both on CPU) for the same Deployment?
Ans. It causes "Thrashing": HPA adds pods to lower average CPU, while VPA increases pod size to handle load, leading to a conflict loop.

Q.30 . Cluster Autoscaler Triggers
Solved
Many users incorrectly assume the Cluster Autoscaler (CA) adds nodes when CPU usage is high. What is the actual specific trigger that causes the CA to provision a new node?
Ans. When a Pod enters the Pending state because no existing node has enough capacity to schedule it.

Q.31 Scale-Up Latency
Solved
In the "Flash Sale" scenario, the HPA scaled the pods instantly, but users still experienced errors for ~8 minutes. What is the primary cause of this delay during the Cluster Autoscaler operations?
Ans. The physical time required to provision a VM (Cloud API), boot the OS, and register the Kubelet.


