Q.33 . Network Topologies and Overhead
Unsolved
You are managing a Kubernetes cluster deployed across multiple distinct physical datacenters. You choose a CNI that utilizes an Overlay network (VXLAN). During a load test, small API requests (GET) succeed instantly, but large database payloads (POST) consistently timeout and drop packets. What is the most likely root cause?
Ans.
 The Pod network interface MTU is set to 1500, failing to account for the ~50-byte VXLAN encapsulation header, causing packet fragmentation failure.

Q.34  Resource Deletion Mechanics
Solved
A junior engineer executes kubectl delete postgresql production-db. Ten minutes later, the resource is still listed when querying the API, but its status shows as Terminating. You inspect the operator's pod logs and find it crashed due to an OutOfMemory (OOM) error an hour ago. What exact API-level mechanism is preventing the resource from disappearing?
Ans.
 The resource's metadata.finalizers array contains a string, instructing the API server to block deletion until the operator explicitly removes the string.

Q.35 Dataplane Isolation Capabilities
Solved
To pass a strict security audit, your team applies a default-deny NetworkPolicy to the finance namespace, intending to block all ingress and egress traffic. However, during a penetration test, pods in the default namespace are still able to curl the finance pods successfully. You verify the YAML is syntactically perfect. What is the architectural reason for this failure?
Ans.
The cluster is utilizing the Flannel CNI, which handles IP allocation and routing but completely ignores NetworkPolicy API objects.

Q.36 Q8. Reconciler State Enforcement
Solved
Your team uses a third-party Operator to manage an Elasticsearch cluster via a Custom Resource. During a live incident, an engineer manually edits the generated Elasticsearch ConfigMap using kubectl edit to increase the JVM heap size and restarts the pods. Three minutes later, the JVM heap size reverts back to the original value, and the pods restart again. What happened?
Ans.
The Operator's reconciliation loop detected that the actual cluster state (the edited ConfigMap) drifted from the desired state (the Custom Resource spec) and overwrote the manual changes.

Q.37API Schema Architecture
Solved
You define a CRD with a field named replicas and specify its type as an integer. A developer attempts to apply a Custom Resource YAML where replicas: "3" (as a string). At what exact stage of the Kubernetes request flow is this resource rejected?
Ans. 
At the API Server validation phase, before the object is ever persisted to etcd or seen by the admission controllers.

Q.38
Your high-frequency trading application requires absolute minimum network latency. You decide to migrate away from a standard routed CNI (which uses iptables and conntrack for packet filtering and NAT) to a CNI like Cilium. What Linux kernel technology does this CNI utilize to achieve near-native packet processing speeds by bypassing the standard network stack?
Ans.
eBPF (Extended Berkeley Packet Filter)

Q.39Large-Scale IP Address Management
Solved
In a cluster with 500 nodes, an autoscaling group abruptly terminates 50 worker nodes without allowing them to gracefully drain. The next day, 50 new nodes join the cluster. However, all new pods scheduled on these nodes are permanently stuck in ContainerCreating. Examining the kubelet logs reveals IPAM: no IPs available in pool. What is the root cause?
ans.
 The CNI suffered from "IP Leaking." Because the old nodes died abruptly, the IPAM plugin never reclaimed their allocated IP blocks, exhausting the cluster's CIDR range.

 Q.40  Subresource Privilege Separation
Solved
You are writing a custom Operator. Your Operator pod successfully reads a Custom Resource, provisions the required Deployments, but throws a 403 Forbidden error when attempting to update the CR's status.phase to "Ready". You verify the Operator's ServiceAccount has [get, list, watch, create, update, patch, delete] verbs for your custom resource group. What is missing?
Ans.
The RoleBinding needs explicit RBAC permissions for the customresources/status subresource; standard permissions only apply to the spec and metadata.

Q.41 A pod attempts to make an HTTP request to http://billing-svc.default.svc.cluster.local. The request fails. You exec into a netshoot debug pod and run nc -zv <billing-svc-cluster-ip> 80, which succeeds. However, running nslookup billing-svc.default fails with a timeout. Which component is fundamentally failing in this scenario?
Ans.
 CoreDNS.


